---
// The research narrative. One continuous page, nine anchored sections, ending at the
// hypothesis — the downstream design is deliberately not on this page.
import ResearchLayout from "@layouts/ResearchLayout.astro";
import ResearchSection from "@components/ResearchSection.astro";
import PaperComparison from "@components/PaperComparison.astro";
import EvidenceFlow from "@components/EvidenceFlow.astro";
import HypothesisPanel from "@components/HypothesisPanel.astro";
import CitationMarker from "@components/CitationMarker.astro";
import ReferenceList from "@components/ReferenceList.astro";
import KeyMessage from "@components/KeyMessage.astro";
import MechanismFigure from "@components/MechanismFigure.astro";
import { relatedWork } from "@data/relatedWork";
import { siteMeta } from "@data/siteMeta";
import { candidateLandscape } from "@data/week001";

const summary = candidateLandscape.summary;
const totalCalls = summary.total_calls.toLocaleString("en-US");
const passSnvs = summary.pass_snvs.toLocaleString("en-US");
---

<ResearchLayout title={siteMeta.title} description={siteMeta.subtitle}>
  <ResearchSection
    id="overview"
    index="01"
    kicker="Research overview"
    title={siteMeta.title}
    lede={siteMeta.subtitle}
  >
    <p>
      The argument below runs from the clinical problem, through what the supplied literature
      establishes, to the tumor-only variant candidates produced so far, and stops at the hypothesis
      those candidates motivate. No detection result is reported, because none has been produced.
    </p>

    <div class="overview-grid">
      <article class="overview-card">
        <h3>The problem</h3>
        <p>
          After treatment, a small number of tumor-derived molecules may remain in a sample that is
          otherwise overwhelmingly non-tumor. Recognising those molecules is the MRD problem.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="overview-card">
        <h3>The supplied literature</h3>
        <p>
          Seven studies and technical documents define the working context: genome-wide signal
          integration, learned background suppression, phased-variant detection, nanopore cell-free
          DNA analysis, consensus long-read multimodal profiling, and native methylation
          <CitationMarker refs={["mrdetect", "mrd-edge", "phased-seq", "ont-realtime", "nanorcs", "methyl-fragmentation", "ont-cfdna-methyl"]} />.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="overview-card">
        <h3>The starting point</h3>
        <p>
          Tumor-only calling with ClairS-TO v0.5.0 <CitationMarker refs="clairs-to" /> on an HCC1395
          nanopore alignment produced {totalCalls} candidate records, of which {passSnvs} are PASS
          SNV candidates.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="overview-card">
        <h3>The hypothesis</h3>
        <p>
          Long reads carry more than the allele at a site. Whether the phase context and the native
          methylation state on the same molecule add discriminative value over SNV evidence alone is
          the open question this project is set up to test.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <KeyMessage title="Work in progress" tone="boundary">
      <p>
        Everything below the ClairS-TO section is a research direction, not a finding. No sensitivity,
        specificity, or limit-of-detection claim is made anywhere on this page, and no comparison
        between the supplied studies' reported performance figures is drawn.
      </p>
    </KeyMessage>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="mrd-problem"
    index="02"
    kicker="Background"
    title="What problem does MRD address?"
  >
    <p>
      <strong>Minimal — or measurable — residual disease</strong> is the small amount of tumor that
      can persist after treatment while remaining invisible to imaging and to routine clinical
      assessment. A patient can appear disease-free and still carry a residual tumor burden that
      determines what happens next.
    </p>
    <p>
      Detecting that residue matters because it is the earliest available evidence about the outcome
      of treatment. It can indicate that disease persists before it becomes clinically apparent, and
      its absence is evidence — not proof — that treatment achieved what it was meant to achieve.
      The measurement is therefore used to inform decisions rather than to describe biology for its
      own sake.
    </p>
    <p>
      The difficulty is one of proportion. Tumor-derived molecules in such a sample are heavily
      outnumbered by molecules from normal tissue. The measurement is not a search for something
      unusual in a neutral background; it is a search for a small number of specific molecules in a
      background that is chemically identical to them in almost every respect and that arrives in
      vast excess.
    </p>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="detection-barriers"
    index="03"
    kicker="Barriers"
    title="Why is MRD detection difficult?"
    lede="Three barriers recur across every method in the supplied literature."
  >
    <div class="barrier-grid">
      <article class="barrier-card">
        <span class="barrier-card__index">1</span>
        <h3>Limited tumor-derived molecules</h3>
        <p>
          At low tumor fraction, the expected number of tumor molecules covering any single position
          becomes small. Evidence that would be convincing in aggregate can be reduced, at one locus,
          to a single observation.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="barrier-card">
        <span class="barrier-card__index">2</span>
        <h3>Sequencing errors can mimic true mutations</h3>
        <p>
          A sequencing or basecalling error produces exactly the observation a rare true mutation
          produces: one read carrying an alternative allele. When true events are rare enough, errors
          can outnumber them.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="barrier-card">
        <span class="barrier-card__index">3</span>
        <h3>Single-modality evidence may be insufficient</h3>
        <p>
          If the only thing recorded about a molecule is which allele it carries, then a true
          observation and a background observation are described by the same single fact, and no
          amount of downstream processing can separate them.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p>
      Stated generally, every method in this space is trying to improve the same ratio:
    </p>

    <p class="display-ratio">
      <span class="display-ratio__num">Tumor signal</span>
      <span class="display-ratio__bar" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="display-ratio__den">Background noise</span>
    </p>

    <KeyMessage title="Background is more than sequencing error" tone="boundary">
      <p>
        In a tumor-only setting the denominator also includes germline variation with no matched
        normal available to subtract it, clonal haematopoiesis and other non-tumor somatic variation,
        alignment and reference artefacts, and — for methylation evidence specifically — bias
        introduced during library preparation <CitationMarker refs="ont-cfdna-methyl" />. Reducing
        sequencing error alone does not make the denominator small.
      </p>
    </KeyMessage>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="related-work"
    index="04"
    kicker="Related work"
    title="What have the supplied studies done?"
    lede="Each study attacks the signal-to-background ratio at a different point in the same pipeline."
  >
    <p>
      The table compares the supplied studies along the axes this project needs: the problem each
      addresses, the evidence it measures, the strategy it proposes, its main contribution, and the
      limitation that matters for a tumor-only long-read setting. Reported performance figures are
      deliberately excluded — the studies use different assays, sample types, and cohorts, and
      placing their numbers side by side would imply a comparison the underlying data does not
      support.
    </p>

    <PaperComparison entries={relatedWork} />
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="synthesis"
    index="05"
    kicker="Synthesis"
    title="Cross-paper synthesis"
    lede="Read together, the studies describe one strategy pursued along different axes."
  >
    <ul class="synthesis-list">
      <li>
        <strong>MRDetect increases genomic breadth</strong> — many weak observations, integrated
        genome-wide, become one usable readout <CitationMarker refs="mrdetect" />.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>MRD-EDGE enriches signal and suppresses noise</strong> — a learned model ranks
        observations by how tumor-like they look before anything is aggregated
        <CitationMarker refs="mrd-edge" />.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>PhasED-Seq uses molecular linkage</strong> — requiring variants to co-occur in phase
        on one molecule makes background fail twice on the same fragment
        <CitationMarker refs="phased-seq" />.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>NanoRCS improves read accuracy and combines modalities</strong> — consensus
        construction raises per-molecule confidence, and several signal classes are estimated together
        <CitationMarker refs="nanorcs" />.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Native nanopore sequencing offers additional molecular information</strong> — genomic,
        fragment-level, and methylation properties are readable from the same native molecule without
        a separate assay <CitationMarker refs={["ont-realtime", "ont-cfdna-methyl"]} />.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Methylation may be biologically relevant but is also technically fragile</strong> —
        it is coupled to fragmentation rather than independent of it
        <CitationMarker refs="methyl-fragmentation" />, and it is sensitive to library preparation
        <CitationMarker refs="ont-cfdna-methyl" />.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The common pattern is that improving the ratio means adding evidence per observation, whether
      that evidence comes from more loci, a learned prior, a second linked variant, or a second
      signal class. Long reads make a particular version of this possible: the allele, its phase
      context, and its native methylation state can be observed on one molecule. The supplied studies
      do not test that specific combination.
    </p>

    <KeyMessage title="The unresolved question" tone="open-question">
      <p>
        Within the supplied literature set, it remains unclear whether phase and native methylation
        evidence add discriminative value to tumor-only long-read SNV candidates under
        low-tumor-fraction conditions.
      </p>
      <p class="key-message__qualifier">
        This is a statement about the seven documents indexed here, not about the research field. A
        broader systematic search has not been completed, so no claim is made that the question is
        unaddressed elsewhere.
      </p>
    </KeyMessage>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="clairs-to"
    index="06"
    kicker="Current output"
    title="Current starting point: ClairS-TO"
    lede="What has actually been produced so far, and what it does and does not establish."
  >
    <EvidenceFlow
      title="Tumor-only candidate generation"
      caption="Conceptual flow of the current output. Counts are call-set sizes, not counts of confirmed somatic mutations."
      steps={[
        { label: "HCC1395 tumor-only ONT BAM", note: "Long-read alignment, no matched normal used as input" },
        { label: "ClairS-TO v0.5.0", note: "Tumor-only somatic candidate calling and filtering" },
        { label: "Total candidate records", value: totalCalls },
        { label: "PASS SNV candidates", value: passSnvs, emphasis: true },
        { label: "Tumor-only candidate landscape", note: "The starting point for everything below" }
      ]}
    />

    <p>
      <code>PASS</code> is a label applied by the caller: it marks the candidates that satisfied
      ClairS-TO's own criteria <CitationMarker refs="clairs-to" />. It is a retention decision, not a
      biological verdict. The retained candidates are not automatically confirmed somatic mutations,
      and the proportion retained is a property of the filtering, not a false-positive rate.
    </p>
    <p>
      What ClairS-TO addresses is candidate calling and filtering in the absence of a matched normal.
      That is a well-defined problem and it is the one solved here. It leaves open a different
      question: given a candidate and the reads supporting it, how much confidence does a single
      alternative-allele observation actually carry?
    </p>

    <KeyMessage title="What this output does not answer" tone="boundary">
      <p>
        The candidate landscape is the current research starting point. It does not tell us whether
        additional phase or methylation evidence would improve recognition of tumor-derived signal,
        because no such evidence has been attached to these candidates yet.
      </p>
    </KeyMessage>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="research-gap"
    index="07"
    kicker="Research gap"
    title="From current output to the hypothesis"
    lede="The reasoning that connects a candidate list to an open research question."
  >
    <EvidenceFlow
      steps={[
        { label: "ClairS-TO provides tumor-only SNV candidates" },
        {
          label:
            "At low tumor fraction, isolated ALT observations may be difficult to distinguish from background"
        },
        {
          label:
            "Long reads may contain additional phase and native methylation information on the same molecule"
        },
        {
          label: "The added discriminative value of these signals is currently unknown",
          emphasis: true
        }
      ]}
      caption="Conceptual reasoning chain. No step here asserts a measured effect."
    />

    <p>
      Each step is a plausibility argument, not a result. The candidates exist; the difficulty at low
      tumor fraction follows from the barriers in section 3; the additional information carried by
      long reads is a property of the platform. Only the last step is genuinely open, and it is open
      in a way that can be tested.
    </p>

    <KeyMessage title="Deliberately not specified here" tone="boundary">
      <p>
        How haplotypes would be assigned, how methylation evidence would be compared, how phase
        blocks would be constructed, how the evidence would be combined into a score, and how the
        alternatives would be evaluated are all design decisions. Making them now, before the
        question is stated cleanly, would fix the answer to the shape of the first implementation.
      </p>
    </KeyMessage>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection
    id="hypothesis"
    index="08"
    kicker="Hypothesis"
    title="Research hypothesis"
  >
    <HypothesisPanel
      question="Does phase evidence and/or native methylation evidence improve the recognition of tumor-derived signal compared with SNV-only evidence?"
      nullHypothesis="Adding phase or native methylation evidence does not improve tumor-signal recognition over SNV-only evidence."
      alternativeHypothesis="Phase and/or native methylation evidence provides additional discriminative value beyond SNV-only evidence."
      scopeNote="The next research stage will define how these evidence modalities should be represented and evaluated. That design is intentionally outside the current website scope."
    />

    <h3 class="figure-heading">Why these two modalities are candidates at all</h3>
    <p>
      The hypothesis rests on one physical fact and one statistical argument. The fact: a long read
      spans enough sequence to carry the candidate allele, the alleles at the neighbouring
      heterozygous sites, and the native methylation calls — all on one molecule, so the candidate
      is observed <em>in cis</em> with its own haplotype background rather than merely near it. The
      argument: those properties are produced by different processes, so the ways they can mislead
      are not the same.
    </p>

    <MechanismFigure>
      <strong>Mechanistic rationale, not evidence.</strong> A somatic variant arises on one
      chromosome copy, so a genuinely tumor-derived read should carry it in cis with the same
      neighbouring alleles every time; an error is free to land on a read from either haplotype, and
      it does not alter that molecule&#39;s methylation state. Whether the separation drawn here
      survives at low tumor fraction is exactly what the hypothesis leaves open.
    </MechanismFigure>

    <KeyMessage title="Why this remains a hypothesis" tone="boundary">
      <p>
        Both axes have known failure modes of their own. Methylation is biologically coupled to
        fragmentation rather than independent of it <CitationMarker refs="methyl-fragmentation" />,
        and it is sensitive to library preparation <CitationMarker refs="ont-cfdna-methyl" />.
        Haplotype context does not exist until phasing succeeds, and phase-based suppression is
        established for co-occurring variants <CitationMarker refs="phased-seq" /> — not for the
        single-variant case examined here. Plausibility is a reason to test, not a result.
      </p>
    </KeyMessage>

    <p class="hypothesis-followup">
      Both outcomes are informative. If the null hypothesis survives, the result is that native
      methylation and phase context are redundant with sequence evidence in this setting — a
      substantive finding about long-read MRD, not a failed experiment. Working notes for the
      preparatory work sit under <a href={siteMeta.researchNotesHref}>research notes</a>.
    </p>
  </ResearchSection>

  <ResearchSection id="references" index="09" kicker="Bibliography" title="References">
    <ReferenceList />
  </ResearchSection>
</ResearchLayout>
